{{Life in Balisca}}

As of 2025, Christianity was determined to be the largest religious group in Balisca. 

Christian denominations in Balisca are usually divided into three large groups: [[Catharism]], [[W:Catholicism|Catholicism]] and [[W:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]]/[[W:Oriental Orthodoxy|Oriental Orthodoxy]]. There are also other Christian denominations, making up a smaller percentage, that do not fall within the confines of these groups, such as [[W:Protestantism|Protestantism]] and various [[W:Restorationism|restorationist]] groups such as the [[W:Latter Day Saint movement|Latter Day Saint movement]], [[W:Adventists|Adventists]] and [[W:Jehovah's Witnesses|Jehovah's Witnesses]].

== History ==

=== Anti-clericalism and anti-Catholicism ===
{{See also|Anti-clericalism|Anti-Catholicism}}
Anti-Catholic sentiment in Balisca has its origins in the early colonial period, rooted in the persecution and violent suppression of [[Catharism|Cathars]] and other heterodox or non-Catholic groups by Xacano Catholic colonists. This tension intensified over centuries into a defining feature of political, cultural, and religious identity in various regions, particularly in [[Cerdanya]], where the descendants of ''rodamóns''—Beron and Volisanian-speaking Cathar refugees—settled after fleeing violence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian Wetlands]], and later the ''Tarracos-''the Cathars that remained in Estuaria.

In the 16th and 17th centuries, the Spanish Catholic authorities systematically targeted Cathar reductions, destroying missions such as ''Sant Domènec de les Missions'' and enslaving large numbers of indigenous allies. This campaign was part of a broader [[Reapers' War]], framed in part as a [[W:Holy war|holy crusade]] to eliminate heretical Christian movements in the colonies. The trauma of this conflict, and the forced displacement of thousands of ''Rodamóns'' to the northern coast of Tinguiririca, left a lasting legacy of bitterness toward Catholic institutions. Although the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian Crown]] had originally intended to use Cathar settlements as a stabilizing counterbalance to [[Great Disappointment|Fosian influence in the New World]], over time the two wings of Volisanian colonial power converged in their repression of non-Catholic movements. This, combined with the perceived hypocrisy and corruption of colonial clergy, hardened a generational mistrust of the Catholic Church in proto-Baliscan society.

==== Baliscan Revolution ====
{{See also|Baliscan Revolution|Right-wing insurgency in Balisca}}
The Revolutionaries which came to power across Balisca in the late 1910s were strongly anti-clerical, secularizing education, prohibiting religious education in the schools, and expelling missionaries from the country. Support for the Catholic Church was weak among the anti-clerical "[[W:Middle-class|middle-class]]" and much of the urban working class, but remained powerful among the wealthy elite in [[Bayara]] and [[Hesperia]]. It had international support from Catholics, especially members of the Italian diaspora, who were politically powerful in neighboring [[Potocsí]]. 

In May 1919 a wave of attacks hit properties of the Catholic Church in [[Macaio]], [[Estuaria]] and [[Levante, Balisca|the Levante]], as dozens of religious buildings, including churches, friaries, convents and schools, lay in ruins. Communes expropriated all Church properties, such as episcopal residences, parish houses, seminaries and monasteries. The Church had to pay rent and taxes in order to continuously use these properties. Religious vestments, chalices, statues, paintings, and similar objects necessary for worship were expropriated as well. In Cerdanya, where memories of colonial violence remained most vivid, revolutionary leaders often invoked the [[Reapers' War#Atrocities|atrocities of the Reapers' War]] in revolutionary propaganda. The Cathar legacy was reimagined not only as a spiritual rebellion, but as an early form of [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialism]], emphasizing spiritual equality, communal ownership, and non-hierarchical structures. Many revolutionary slogans evoked Cathar principles—''Només Déu pot jutjar-nos'' ("Only God may judge us")—while rejecting the authority of any earthly clergy.

Many Catholics fought back by forming – for the first time – Catholic armed militias, the ''[[Warriors of Christ the King|Guerrilleros de Cristo Rey]]'' (GCR). It was dissolved in 1930.

